(e) provide the Contractor with reasonable assistance in obtaining any third-party consents or approvals required for the performance of the Services.

5.2 The Authority may issue directions to the Contractor from time to time in relation to the performance of the Services. The Contractor shall comply with any such direction, provided that it is reasonable and consistent with the terms of this Contract.

6. MAINTENANCE STANDARDS

6.1 The Contractor shall perform the Services in accordance with the Maintenance Standards set out in Schedule 3.

6.2 The Maintenance Standards include, but are not limited to, standards relating to:

(a) track geometry, including gauge, alignment, and cross-level;

(b) rail defects, including internal and surface defects;

(c) sleepers, ballast, and formation condition;

(d) signalling and level crossing equipment;

(e) bridges, culverts, and other structures;

(f) vegetation management and drainage; and

(g) environmental management, including the handling and disposal of waste materials.

6.3 The Contractor shall notify the Authority in writing of any proposed deviation from the Maintenance Standards, together with the reasons for the proposed deviation. The Contractor shall not deviate from the Maintenance Standards without the prior written approval of the Authority.

6.4 The Authority may, from time to time, amend the Maintenance Standards by written notice to the Contractor. Any such amendment shall be reasonable and shall take into account the impact on the Contractor’s ability to perform the Services. Where an amendment results in a material increase or decrease in the Contractor’s costs, the Contract Price shall be adjusted in accordance with clause 12.

(d) the number and qualifications of personnel to be deployed;

(e) a risk assessment identifying the hazards associated with the proposed activity and the control measures to be implemented;

(f) the proposed traffic management arrangements, including any temporary speed restrictions or possession requirements;

(g) the proposed arrangements for the protection of the public, including fencing, signage, and lookout persons; and

(h) any other information reasonably requested by the Authority.

7.4 The Authority may approve a proposed work method with or without conditions, or may refuse to approve it. The Authority shall notify the Contractor in writing of its decision within ten (10) Business Days of receiving a complete submission under clause 7.2. If the Authority refuses to approve a proposed work method, it shall provide reasons for its decision.

7.5 The Contractor shall comply strictly with each Approved Work Method. The Contractor shall not vary an Approved Work Method without the prior written approval of the Authority. Any request for variation shall be made in accordance with clause 7.2 and 7.3, and the Authority shall consider the request in accordance with clause 7.4.

7.6 The Authority may withdraw or amend an Approved Work Method by written notice to the Contractor where the Authority reasonably considers that the Approved Work Method no longer provides for the safe or efficient performance of the Services. The Contractor shall comply with any such notice, provided that where the withdrawal or amendment results in a material change to the Contractor's obligations or costs, the Contract Price shall be adjusted in accordance with clause 12.

7.7 Without limiting the generality of the foregoing, the following work methods have been approved by the Authority as at the date of this Contract:

(a) **Track Replacement Using Continuous Welded Rail.** Southern Maintenance Services has_approved_work_method track replacement using continuous welded rail. Record status: current. This approval is current as at the date of this Contract and is subject to the conditions set out in Schedule 4.

(b) **Ballast Undercutting and Cleaning.** Approved for use on formation renewal works within the defined scope of the Services, subject to the conditions set out in Schedule 4.

(c) **Rail Grinding.** Approved for use on corrective and preventive rail profile maintenance, subject to the conditions set out in Schedule 4.

7.8 The conditions referred to in clause 7.7 include, without limitation:

(a) for track replacement using continuous welded rail:

(i) the Contractor shall use rail that meets the specifications set out in Schedule 3, including the grade, section, and metallurgical properties specified therein;

(ii) the Contractor shall conduct a pre-works survey of the rail temperature and shall not commence welding or stressing operations when the rail temperature is outside the range specified in the approved method;

(iii) the Contractor shall provide the Authority with a copy of the welding procedure qualification record for each welding crew at least seven (7) days prior to the commencement of the relevant works;

(iv) the Contractor shall carry out non-destructive testing of all welds in accordance with the frequency and methods specified in the approved method, and shall provide the results of such testing to the Authority within five (5) Business Days of the completion of the testing;

**8. INSURANCE**

8.1 The Contractor shall effect and maintain, at its own cost, the following insurances in respect of the Services, from the Commencement Date until the expiry of the Defects Liability Period:

(a) **Public Liability Insurance** – with an indemnity limit of not less than Twenty Million Dollars ($20,000,000) per claim, inclusive of legal costs, in respect of any one occurrence or series of occurrences arising out of one event;

(b) **Workers Compensation Insurance** – as required by the Workplace Injury Rehabilitation and Compensation Act 2013 (Vic) and any other applicable legislation;

(c) **Contract Works Insurance** – in respect of the physical loss of or damage to the Services and any materials, plant, or equipment to be incorporated into the Services, for an amount not less than the full replacement value thereof, with an excess not exceeding Fifty Thousand Dollars ($50,000) per claim;

(d) **Motor Vehicle Insurance** – comprehensive cover for all vehicles used in connection with the Services;

(e) **Professional Indemnity Insurance** – with an indemnity limit of not less than Five Million Dollars ($5,000,000) per claim, in respect of any negligent act, error, or omission in the performance of the Services.

8.2 Each policy of insurance required under clause 8.1 shall:

(a) be effected with an insurer of good repute and holding an Australian Financial Services Licence;

(b) note the interest of the Authority and the State of Victoria in the policy, where the policy is capable of noting such interest;

(c) provide that the insurer shall not cancel, materially alter, or fail to renew the policy without giving the Authority not less than thirty (30) days’ prior written notice;

(d) be maintained in full force and effect, and the Contractor shall not do or omit to do any act or thing which would entitle the insurer to avoid, repudiate, or reduce liability under the policy.

8.3 The Contractor shall provide to the Authority, upon request, certificates of currency or other reasonable evidence of the insurances required under this clause 8, including evidence of payment of premiums.

8.4 The Contractor shall ensure that each subcontractor engaged in connection with the Services effects and maintains insurances consistent with the requirements of this clause 8, and shall procure a right for the Authority to inspect such policies or certificates of currency.

8.5 Without limiting clause 8.4, the Contractor shall ensure that any subcontractor engaged in connection with the Services is noted as a joint insured under the Contractor’s Public Liability Insurance policy, or alternatively effects its own policy with equivalent coverage and limits.

**9. PERSONNEL AND RESOURCES**

9.1 The Contractor shall provide all personnel, plant, equipment, materials, and other resources necessary for the performance of the Services, including:

(a) a suitably qualified and experienced Project Manager, who shall be the single point of contact for the Authority in relation to the day-to-day delivery of the Services;

(b) such supervisors, engineers, track workers, and other personnel as are necessary to perform the Services in accordance with this Contract;

(c) all plant and equipment required to perform the Services, including but not limited to tampers, regulators, rail grinders, cranes, and hi-rail vehicles;

(d) all materials required for the performance of the Services, including but not limited to ballast, sleepers, rail, fastenings, and welding consumables.

11. TERMINATION

11.1 The Authority may terminate this Contract by written notice to the Contractor if:

(a) the Contractor abandons the Services or otherwise demonstrates an intention not to be bound by this Contract;

(b) the Contractor fails to proceed with the Services with due expedition and without delay, and such failure is not remedied within fourteen (14) days of written notice from the Superintendent requiring the Contractor to remedy the failure;

(c) the Contractor fails to comply with a direction of the Superintendent under clause 8 (Quality Management and Inspection) or clause 9 (Safety and Environmental Obligations), and such failure is not remedied within fourteen (14) days of written notice from the Superintendent;

(d) the Contractor suspends the performance of the Services, in whole or in part, otherwise than in accordance with this Contract;

(e) the Contractor is in breach of clause 6 (Insurances) or clause 7 (Security);

(f) the Contractor becomes insolvent, or enters into any arrangement or composition with its creditors, or has a receiver, administrator, or liquidator appointed over any of its assets, or takes or suffers any analogous action under any applicable law;

(g) the Contractor or any of its subcontractors engages in fraudulent, corrupt, or collusive conduct in connection with this Contract, including but not limited to conduct prohibited under the *Independent Broad-based Anti-corruption Commission Act 2011* (Vic);

(h) an event of default occurs under any security provided under clause 7; or

(i) the Contractor assigns or purports to assign this Contract or any benefit or interest under it, otherwise than in accordance with clause 17 (Assignment and Subcontracting).

11.2 Where the Authority terminates this Contract under clause 11.1, the Authority may:

(a) take possession of all plant, materials, and equipment on the Site or elsewhere that are the property of the Contractor or in respect of which the Contractor has a right of possession, and use such plant, materials, and equipment to complete the Services or to procure the completion of the Services by others;

(b) withhold payment of any amount otherwise due to the Contractor until the Services have been completed and the cost of completing the Services has been determined;

(c) recover from the Contractor all costs and expenses reasonably incurred by the Authority in completing the Services and in remedying any damage or loss caused by the Contractor's default, including any additional costs incurred by the Authority in procuring the Services from another contractor; and

(d) exercise any other rights or remedies available to the Authority at law or in equity.

11.3 In the event of termination under clause 11.1, the Contractor shall:

(a) immediately cease all work under this Contract, except as directed by the Superintendent for the purpose of protecting the Site, the Works, or the safety of persons or property;

(b) deliver to the Superintendent all drawings, records, test results, and other documents relating to the Services, whether in hard copy or electronic form;

(c) remove from the Site all plant, materials, and equipment owned by the Contractor or its subcontractors within a reasonable time of being directed by the Superintendent to do so; and

(d) provide all reasonable assistance to the Authority and any replacement contractor to facilitate the orderly transfer of the Services.
